      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Georg Thieme Verlag Stuttgart ˙ New York.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Hemoperitoneum due to spontaneous rupture of a visceral vessel may result from a variety of underlying pathologies. However, its idiopathic form does not show evidence of any predisposition."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENT AND METHODS",
            "content": "An abdominal CT scan for acute abdominal pain yielded the unexpected diagnosis of a ruptured splenic artery in a 21-year-old patient. Hemostasis was achieved by endovascular coiling of a segmental splenic artery lacking any evidence of pathological transformation. An extensive intraabdominal hematoma was evacuated in a consecutive laparoscopy which, furthermore, confirmed interventional success. Despite extensive diagnostic efforts, the cause of the bleeding remained undefined."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Spontaneous hemoperitoneum may occasionally be considered as a cause of acute abdomial pain and is diagnostically challenging. An interdisciplinary approach is desirable to meet the objective of modern organ-preserving therapy in splenic artery rupture."
          }
        ]
      },
